---
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import Eyebrow from "@/components/Eyebrow.astro";
import LabelledBand from "@/components/LabelledBand.astro";

/**
 * The claim the footer makes, written out.
 *
 * The footer has said "designed to meet WCAG 2.1 AA" since the site went up,
 * which is a promise with nowhere to read it. This is the page it points at:
 * what was built in, what was measured, what is still weak, and how to say so.
 *
 * It is deliberately specific. A statement that says "we care about
 * accessibility" tells a reader nothing they can check, and this site's whole
 * argument is that an honest absence beats a confident-looking blank. The same
 * rule applies to its own workings, so the limitations are named rather than
 * implied and the numbers are the ones actually measured.
 */
const body = "text-body text-ink-muted max-w-[760px] space-y-5";
const list = "text-body text-ink-muted max-w-[760px] list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 marker:text-ink-faint";
---

<Base
  title="Accessibility — Dub Digger"
  description="How Dub Digger is built for assistive technology, what was measured against WCAG 2.1 AA, and what is still weak."
  pageType="WebPage"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Accessibility", href: "/accessibility" }]}
>
  <div class="column pt-16 pb-16 md:pt-24">
    <Eyebrow>Accessibility statement</Eyebrow>
    <h1 class="text-name text-ink-strong mt-6 leading-[0.92] font-bold tracking-[-0.04em]">
      Accessibility<span class="text-accent">.</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="text-lead text-ink-muted mt-10 max-w-[760px]">
      I want Dub Digger to be usable by as many people as possible, including anyone using a screen
      reader, a keyboard on its own, or a browser turned up to a size that suits them. It is
      designed and built to meet WCAG 2.1 Level AA.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="column pb-24">
    <LabelledBand label="Where things stand">
      <div class={body}>
        <p>
          The site was audited against WCAG 2.1 Level AA in August 2026, and the problems that
          audit found were fixed. Contrast was measured rather than eyeballed, and every page was
          checked for landmarks, heading order and keyboard reach.
        </p>
        <p>
          I did that testing myself. Nobody independent has evaluated this site, and it has not
          been tested end to end with a real screen reader by someone who uses one daily. So treat
          this as a careful self-assessment, not a certificate.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is also a beta, and it changes often. If something worked last month and does not
          now, that is worth telling me about.
        </p>
      </div>
    </LabelledBand>

    <LabelledBand label="What is built in">
      <ul class={list}>
        <li>
          A skip link as the first thing on every page, so the header, the search field and the
          navigation can be jumped in one keystroke.
        </li>
        <li>
          One main region and one first-level heading per page, with the header, the navigation and
          the footer marked as regions around it. Both navigation bars carry their own name, so they
          are told apart rather than read as two of the same thing.
        </li>
        <li>
          Everything is reachable and operable by keyboard, in the order it appears on screen.
          Focus is always visible, marked by a two-pixel accent outline held clear of the element.
        </li>
        <li>
          Body text sits at 4.7:1 against the background or better, and the boundary of anything you
          can click clears 3:1. The quietest grey on the site is the one measured hardest, because
          it carries the counts and the column headings.
        </li>
        <li>
          The phone menu is a native disclosure. It announces its own state, closes on Escape, and
          keeps the keyboard inside it while it is open without hiding the page underneath from a
          screen reader.
        </li>
        <li>
          Where a column heading folds underneath a name on a narrow screen, it stays available to a
          screen reader on a wide one. A row should never be read out as four values with nothing
          saying what they count.
        </li>
        <li>
          Every icon is decoration and is hidden from assistive technology. There are no images in
          the site's content at all, so there is no unlabelled picture carrying meaning.
        </li>
        <li>
          A link that leaves the site says so in its accessible name before you follow it.
        </li>
        <li>
          If your system asks for reduced motion, it is respected. Nothing slides, animates or
          counts up, and you still get the real figures.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </LabelledBand>

    <LabelledBand label="It works without JavaScript">
      <div class={body}>
        <p>
          Search is a plain form. The tabs, the sort control and "Load more" are ordinary links that
          carry their state in the address. So every view is a real URL you can bookmark, share or go
          back to, and the whole site works with scripting switched off or failing to load.
        </p>
        <p>
          The few things that do use JavaScript improve a page rather than build it. The bio on an
          artist page collapses only if the script runs; without it you get the whole thing, which is
          the right way round.
        </p>
      </div>
    </LabelledBand>

    <LabelledBand label="No third-party content">
      <div class={body}>
        <p>
          This site loads nothing from anyone else. No trackers, no cookies, no ads, no embeds, no
          consent banner to dismiss before you can read anything. The typefaces are served from the
          same machine as the pages.
        </p>
        <p>
          That matters here beyond privacy: third-party widgets are a common source of accessibility
          problems nobody on the site can fix. There are none to fix.
        </p>
      </div>
    </LabelledBand>

    <LabelledBand label="Where it is still weak">
      <div class={body}>
        <p>
          The interface is dense on purpose. It is built for someone who reads Discogs pages for
          fun and wants a lot of information per screen, and that is a choice which suits some
          people and not others. Long lists are paged at forty rows to keep that manageable.
        </p>
        <p>
          The corpus text comes from Discogs, written by thousands of volunteers. Artist names,
          biographies and credit roles arrive as they were typed, so their wording, punctuation and
          capitalisation are not mine to make consistent.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some artist names are very long and set very large. They should reflow rather than
          overflow at any width, but that is the part I would most expect to have missed a case in.
        </p>
      </div>
    </LabelledBand>

    <LabelledBand label="Tell me if something is off">
      <div class={body}>
        <p>
          If you hit an obstacle using this site, or you want to report an accessibility problem,
          please get in touch through <a
            href="https://simoneferraro.co.uk"
            rel="noreferrer"
            class="link-rule">simoneferraro.co.uk</a
          >. I will do my best to answer and to fix it.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is useful, but not necessary, to know which page you were on, what you were using to
          read it, and what you expected to happen.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink-faint font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.14em] uppercase">
          Last reviewed August 2026
        </p>
      </div>
    </LabelledBand>
  </div>
</Base>
